&lt;p&gt;Prices are as listed on each product's own pages in October 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The large portals
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;LinkedIn&lt;/strong&gt; has the widest set of jobs and the most recruiters. You create a job alert from any search. By default it sends a daily email plus app notifications, and you can switch the email to weekly. Email alerts are a digest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Naukri&lt;/strong&gt; is strong for IT services and large employers. You save an alert from a search, and per Naukri's FAQ it's emailed a few times a week. App notifications are configurable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Profiles that recruiters find
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instahyre&lt;/strong&gt; is free for job seekers. It curates openings for your profile and lets matched recruiters contact you. It's often recommended for mid-level engineers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cutshort&lt;/strong&gt; is free for candidates and focused on tech and product roles. You build a profile, and Cutshort matches it to employers and shows recommended jobs. It's strong for startups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Wellfound&lt;/strong&gt; (formerly AngelList Talent) is free for candidates, with email job alerts and many India and remote startup roles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hirist&lt;/strong&gt; is a free, tech-only job board for India with an Android app. It's useful when you want listings without non-tech noise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Search everything at once
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Google job search&lt;/strong&gt; shows a jobs panel gathered from many sites when you search for a role. Signed in, you can turn on email alerts for that search. It's good for breadth; you still apply on the original site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Built for speed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prefetch&lt;/strong&gt; reads company career pages and ATS boards (Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby and others) for engineering roles. It says it checks about every 15 minutes, and it alerts by email. Paid plans start at ₹99 a month.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Jobrix&lt;/strong&gt; sends ranked job alerts on Telegram. Its free tier lists up to 10 alerts a day with a 2-hour delay. Pro, at ₹349 a month, lists faster alerts, more alerts a day and AI fit explanations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Jobspiq (mine)&lt;/strong&gt; checks multiple sources about every 30 minutes: job portals and employer hiring systems such as Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby and Workday. It scores each new tech job in India against your skills and experience, showing the skills you have and the ones you're missing. Strong matches go to the Android app, your browser or Telegram, usually within a minute of Jobspiq finding them, and email comes as a daily digest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my own measurement of jobs posted in the 14 days to 12 October 2026, the median time from an employer publishing a job to Jobspiq finding it was about 6 minutes on Greenhouse (107 jobs) and 12 minutes on Ashby (89 jobs). The &lt;a href="https://jobspiq.in/blog/direct-ats-indexing-vs-job-board-noise" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;method is here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Jobspiq also includes an application tracker and resume tailoring. Every feature is free during the beta, with no card needed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where others are better:&lt;/strong&gt; LinkedIn and Naukri have far more jobs and recruiters. Instahyre and Cutshort bring recruiters to you, which Jobspiq doesn't do for candidates. Jobspiq covers tech jobs in India only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to choose
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fresher or early career:&lt;/strong&gt; Naukri and LinkedIn for volume, plus fast alerts for product companies.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mid-level, and you want recruiters to come to you:&lt;/strong&gt; Instahyre or Cutshort, plus LinkedIn.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Targeting specific product companies:&lt;/strong&gt; a service that reads ATS boards, with push or Telegram alerts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Remote roles:&lt;/strong&gt; Wellfound and remote filters, checking that each job is open to candidates in India.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


More details
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full comparison, with links to each product's own help and pricing pages, is here: &lt;a href="https://jobspiq.in/blog/job-alert-apps-for-developers-india-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Job Alert Apps for Developers in India (2026)&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Products change their features and prices often, so check each site before you decide. If something here is out of date, tell me in the comments and I'll fix it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; For a gaming supplier-invoice pipeline, upscale the generated image before field extraction only when the source is readable but too small. Send the base64 data or URL from the generation response to &lt;code&gt;POST /v1/ai/image/upscale&lt;/code&gt;, then save or stream the returned image. The runtime supports Lanczos only, so this is a focused preprocessing step, not a general image-enhancement system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The decision is about structured output correctness. A bigger image does not make a wrong invoice number right. It can, however, give the extraction step more pixels to work with when a line item or footer total is cramped.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the original.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What problem are we solving?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The source image is generated from text. The next step is to extract fields from a supplier invoice used by a gaming business: supplier name, invoice number, currency, line items, tax, and total. A small image can make those fields harder for a downstream extraction step to read, especially when a line item is dense or the total sits in a low-contrast footer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would separate two jobs. Image enlargement changes pixels. Field extraction decides data. Mixing them makes debugging expensive because a wrong total can come from the image, the parser, or a business rule. My revenue-per-hour test is blunt: if enlargement is a small, repeatable preprocessing step, outsource it to the runtime and spend engineering time on invoice validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai fits that boundary when a plain REST call is more useful than adding another client library. A Node.js process can send an HTTP request with the generated image payload; there is no SDK version to maintain for this step. The second practical advantage is operational: one key and one bill can cover the surrounding backend capabilities, instead of a separate credential and reconciliation task for every service in the invoice workflow. Its public discovery surface is self-describing, and documented capabilities include runnable examples in 10 languages, which shortens the handoff from route discovery to a working integration. The broader surface contains 295 routes across 20 modules under one key. That reduces integration friction, but it does not remove the need to validate the extracted fields.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The self-describing discovery surface is a second, different advantage for this workflow. Before wiring the invoice path, a small team can inspect the capability schema without first obtaining another client package or guessing at a payload. The 10-language examples also make a handoff less dependent on the original author. That matters when the weekly shipping window is measured in hours, not a platform team's sprint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai also offers the one key, one bill model as a practical boundary: one credential covers the capabilities this workflow grows into, so adding validation does not automatically add another key and another invoice to reconcile. Its breadth is a separate advantage: one platform covers 295 routes across 20 modules behind a consistent interface, so the invoice path can grow without turning every new backend capability into a new integration convention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The operational advantage is a single key and unified billing across the backend capabilities in use. This self-describing platform has a public discovery surface, so the invoice path can inspect a capability before wiring it in. Generation, image preprocessing, and later validation do not each need a new credential convention or a separate monthly reconciliation trail. It is a small administrative win, but small wins compound when one person owns the whole product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is plain. The operation is Lanczos-only, so a workflow that needs several resampling algorithms needs another component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why choose Lanczos for this invoice path?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lanczos is a resampling filter, not an invoice reader. It can increase pixel dimensions, but it cannot recover information that was never present or guarantee that a blurry glyph becomes the correct digit. A larger bad input is still a bad input.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For generated supplier invoices, I would use it before extraction when the original output is visually legible but too small for the next model or renderer. I would not treat it as a repair step for a malformed invoice. The fixed algorithm is also a useful product rule. Fewer knobs mean fewer per-document decisions, which helps a one-person SaaS ship weekly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where the 2026 runtime snapshot matters: upscale is one capability in the &lt;code&gt;ai-runtime&lt;/code&gt; group, and the verified limitation is algorithm-specific, not a promise of arbitrary enhancement. Keep the original image beside the processed one until extraction and validation finish. That gives the review path two artifacts instead of one ambiguous result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do I call the upscale route from Node.js?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The only route needed here is &lt;code&gt;POST /v1/ai/image/upscale&lt;/code&gt;. The initial generation response supplies the input as base64 data or a URL. Read the route-specific request schema from the runtime discovery surface before constructing the payload; the verified facts for this workflow do not define a field name, so inventing one would make a copy-paste example misleading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The wrapper below accepts an already-shaped image payload. It uses environment variables for the API base URL and key, sends an explicit method, retries a rate-limit response with &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt;, and reports non-success responses instead of treating every response as an image. Configure &lt;code&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL&lt;/code&gt; with the versioned API base before running it. Four attempts is a bounded retry policy, not a promise that the image operation is safe to repeat without an idempotent caller policy.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ImagePayload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;retryDelayMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;retry-after&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;NaN&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isFinite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;8000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;upscaleInvoiceImage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ImagePayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL and INFRAI_API_KEY are required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/ai/image/upscale`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;content-type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;retryDelayMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Upscale failed (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Upscale failed after rate-limit retries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Pass the base64 data or URL captured from the generation response in the exact request shape published for this route. Save the returned high-resolution image or stream it to the client. Keep the original beside it until extraction and field validation finish; this preserves evidence when a supplier disputes an invoice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How does it compare with other choices?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are three sensible image-processing alternatives, and each moves responsibility to a different place. Sharp, ImageMagick, and Cloudinary are image-processing options, not invoice extraction models. At the later extraction boundary, OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google Gemini are separate alternatives with their own model, schema, and data-handling choices. That distinction keeps the comparison honest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Strength&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Boundary for this workflow&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sharp&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A Node.js image-processing library built around libvips&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Good when resizing should run locally; the service owns the dependency and its operation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ImageMagick&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A broad command-line image toolkit with many operations and filters&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Good when an existing worker already standardizes it; broader control brings more deployment and tuning surface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloudinary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A hosted media transformation service with URL-based transformations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Good when managed media delivery already exists; it adds a separate media system and delivery model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A plain REST runtime call&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One HTTP boundary from the Node.js service, with Lanczos fixed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Good for a small integration when the algorithm limit is acceptable; poor for per-image filter selection&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google Gemini belong in the extraction comparison, not in the resampling row. OpenAI is a fit when the application already standardizes on its multimodal API; Anthropic is a fit when its document and vision workflow is the chosen model boundary; Gemini is a fit when the surrounding stack already uses Google tooling. None of those choices changes the pixel-resampling limitation here, so adding one does not eliminate the need to test supplier, invoice number, line items, tax, and total.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is not a benchmark. It is an ownership decision. Sharp keeps pixels close to Node. ImageMagick gives a mature toolbox. Cloudinary handles a wider media workflow. The REST option keeps this preprocessing action beside the rest of an API-driven application, with one credential convention and a single billing relationship for the platform capabilities already in use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For supplier invoices, structured output correctness should decide. If a representative test set shows that Lanczos produces readable inputs and extraction passes field validation, the small boundary wins. If the set exposes ringing around small digits or the product requires algorithm-specific tuning, choose the component that exposes that control. Do not choose on a price table alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first version can be synchronous: receive the generated image, upscale it, hand the result to extraction, and return validated fields. That is enough to ship a narrow workflow. Record the original image reference, upscale response, and extraction decision so a bad invoice can be replayed. The measure is not “looks sharper.” It is correct supplier, invoice number, line items, tax, and total.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At higher volume, move the work behind a queue and make the consumer idempotent. The same supplier invoice should not create two records because a client retried after a network timeout. A 429 response is a transport event, not an image-quality result, so the client should honor &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt; and avoid a tight retry loop. Retain a small corpus of representative invoices and compare extracted fields before changing preprocessing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the runtime exposes Lanczos only, keep &lt;code&gt;resampling_algorithm&lt;/code&gt; out of product configuration unless the service later supports it. Configuration that cannot change behavior is a trap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the Lanczos route when the generated invoice image needs a larger pixel surface, the input can be supplied as base64 data or a URL, and one fixed resampling method is enough. Keep extraction validation separate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use Sharp or ImageMagick when local ownership and filter control matter more than a small HTTP integration. Use Cloudinary when transformation is already part of a managed media delivery system. In all cases, judge the result by structured invoice fields, not by a prettier thumbnail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sharp documentation: &lt;a href="https://sharp.pixelplumbing.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://sharp.pixelplumbing.com/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ImageMagick resize documentation: &lt;a href="https://imagemagick.org/script/command-line-options.php#resize" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://imagemagick.org/script/command-line-options.php#resize&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cloudinary image transformations: &lt;a href="https://cloudinary.com/documentation/image_transformations" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://cloudinary.com/documentation/image_transformations&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Node.js Fetch API: &lt;a href="https://nodejs.org/api/globals.html#fetch" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://nodejs.org/api/globals.html#fetch&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lanczos resampling overview: &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lanczos_resampling" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lanczos_resampling&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;By Bo&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A monitoring path that depends on a working AI session can disappear exactly when an operator needs it. In my operating notes, provider-authentication failures prevented AI agent turns from completing. That exposed a useful boundary: routine infrastructure checks should keep running, while AI helps investigate exceptions when it is available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is an operating pattern, not a claim that I have deployed or benchmarked a complete monitoring fleet. I have no measured uptime or cost improvement to report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Keep collection independent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The regular collector should have a small, bounded job: check expected hosts and services, record observations, evaluate explicit rules, and report changes. It should not wait for a model response. A provider timeout or authentication error should affect enrichment, not erase a disk-space result or suppress an alert.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a local service check, the diagnostic shape can be small. These are illustrative local inspections, not a deployed fleet collector:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;systemctl --user show agent-gateway.service -p ActiveState -p SubState -p NRestarts&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;df -P /&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;free -m&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The unit name is an example. These commands do not establish remote reachability, messaging delivery, or application correctness. Each check should report what it actually observed and when.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Give AI an optional, bounded role
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a check changes or fails, a separate triage step can pass the assistant a compact evidence packet: the changed state, timestamps, relevant log excerpts, and the previous observation. Healthy routine checks do not need a model-generated paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The triage result should separate observed facts, possible causes, and a suggested next diagnostic. Each explanation should point to evidence. If the packet lacks a needed observation, the assistant should say what is missing rather than fill the gap with confidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A provider error belongs in the triage status. It should be visible as “analysis unavailable,” not converted to “host healthy,” and it should not block deterministic checks. Timeouts and bounded retries keep a slow model call from backing up collection. The collector and alert path need their own health signals so an old green result cannot look current and a failed notification channel cannot go unnoticed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Track distinct failure states
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An unreachable host, a collector that failed before checking it, and a stale observation are different outcomes. I would retain a host alias, collection time, check name, result, and evidence reference for each observation. This makes it possible to see whether the system checked and found a problem, or never completed the check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same separation matters for services. A process can be running while its application channel is unauthenticated. A channel can be healthy while a model backend is unavailable. In my incident notes, resource exhaustion and a messaging-credential problem both appeared to users as a silent bot, even though they needed different checks. A later runtime change also showed that a new executable on disk does not prove the running service has switched to it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those cases argue for distinct signals, not universal thresholds. Start with a small inventory of hosts and services whose ownership and expected state are known. Add capacity only after “checked,” “failed to check,” and “stale” are easy to distinguish.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Keep actions behind a decision
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Monitoring can identify a capacity problem without authorizing cleanup. In one maintenance session, storage was nearly full, but the largest consumers were protected workspaces and backups. The cleanup stopped instead of deleting those directories to meet a target.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a useful boundary for an AI-assisted operator: detection can end with “issue confirmed; owner decision required.” A recommendation should name its target, evidence, expected effect, and recovery plan. It should not restart a service, delete files, or upgrade packages by implication. Keep monitoring access read-only where possible, and treat text found in logs as data rather than instructions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Measure after deployment
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The design I would start with is modest: deterministic collection, visible freshness, an alert route independent of AI, and optional analysis of exceptions. Track collection completion, stale observations, alert delivery, and model-triage availability separately. Measure actual provider usage and operating costs after deployment instead of promising savings in advance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the model is offline, the system should still tell the operator what it checked, what changed, and which action remains pending. AI can help explain an exception; it should not be the only thing keeping watch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;A personal author post about LongCat-DeepResearch, not an official team announcement.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask a deep research system a real question and watch what happens inside it. The question splits into sub-questions. One source contradicts another. A promising lead turns out to be a press release repeating the same unsourced number. By the time the system starts writing, its context holds search results, abandoned branches, half-finished paragraphs, and the original request, all competing for the same window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the problem LongCat-DeepResearch is built around. In a new technical report, my co-authors and I describe a system that separates &lt;em&gt;global coordination&lt;/em&gt; from &lt;em&gt;local investigation&lt;/em&gt;. The claim is deliberately modest: on three public benchmarks, treating each compared product as a complete system with its native tools and budgets, it scores highest among the four systems compared — but the comparison does not isolate model quality, and several development studies are not independent confirmation. I'm one of the authors; this is a personal explanation of the work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The context problem is an architecture problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most agent designs keep one expanding history. Evidence, intermediate reasoning, and draft text accumulate together. Compressing that history early is risky: you may discard a detail before you know whether it matters. Keeping everything is expensive and eventually impossible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LongCat-DeepResearch instead makes the shared state small and inspectable. Planners briefly search and read &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; committing to a plan. Several planning writers propose candidate specifications; a judge merges their perspectives; a critic looks for missing questions and cases; a reviser produces the final &lt;strong&gt;ResearchSpec&lt;/strong&gt;. That document records scope, research questions, required entities or cases, provisional source leads, and who owns which section. The detailed evidence stays with the researcher that will use it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgpphy92ujxyl8yxgwsxd.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgpphy92ujxyl8yxgwsxd.png" alt="Figure 1. Public-benchmark scores for the four compared systems. Source: LongCat-DeepResearch project page." width="800" height="348"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Figure 1. Public-benchmark scores. These compare deployed systems with their native tools and budgets, not isolated model components. Source: &lt;a href="https://meituan-longcat.github.io/LongCat-DeepResearch/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;LongCat-DeepResearch project page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each researcher receives the original query, the complete ResearchSpec, and exactly one assignment. It gathers evidence in its own context and writes a cited section from that history. The harness then assembles the completed sections &lt;em&gt;mechanically&lt;/em&gt;, without asking a final writer to regenerate the report. A global editor identifies conflicts, repetition, and ownership problems; local editors revise only their assigned sections. The original section artifacts remain available for inspection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://meituan-longcat.github.io/LongCat-DeepResearch/assets/research-harness-arxiv-2609.36071.svg" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;View Figure 2: the LongCat-DeepResearch harness&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Figure 2. The research harness. Planning produces an executable ResearchSpec; independent researchers write citation-bearing sections; assembly is followed by global-to-local editing. Source: &lt;a href="https://meituan-longcat.github.io/LongCat-DeepResearch/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;project page&lt;/a&gt;, figure from the technical report.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the evaluation does and does not show
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On DeepResearchBench, DeepResearchBench II, and ResearchRubrics, the reported scores are 55.25, 51.35, and 79.83. The strongest of the three compared products scores 54.95, 48.18, and 74.21 on the same benchmarks, so the recorded gaps are +0.30, +3.17, and +5.62 points. On a separate in-house benchmark the system scores 76.04 overall — second among the four, 0.55 points behind ChatGPT-DeepResearch, while leading on that benchmark's Content and Analysis scores.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The more interesting evidence is a small ablation. Holding the previous LongCat model fixed and swapping in the current harness moves the three scores from 47.65 / 33.13 / 60.33 to 53.58 / 48.68 / 71.90. Holding the harness fixed and using the current model moves them to 55.25 / 51.35 / 79.83. Model capability and orchestration look complementary rather than substitutable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Component studies point the same way, with caveats. On a fixed ResearchRubrics development subset, integrating three planning writers through the judge raises the final total from 81.67 to 85.13 versus a single-writer plan. Replacing parallel section researchers with one whole-report researcher lowers both displayed benchmark means. But more planning refinement is not automatically better: critic/reviser cycles raise planned coverage while report scores move differently. The ResearchSpec is inspectable state, not a calibrated selector for the best report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Scope, limits, and one traceable case
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The public numbers compare complete products, so they cannot attribute the result to one model component or training stage. The readability study is model-judged, not a human rating. Component and editor analyses use previously inspected development subsets, so their trends are not independent full-benchmark confirmation. And in the current pipeline the ResearchSpec is fixed after dispatch; reopening the global agenda mid-research remains future work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A recorded portfolio-management case shows both the value and the limit of traceability. The merged ResearchSpec assigned 19 subsections across four themes. The critic noticed that Deep Portfolio Theory was missing, and the reviser added a concrete question to one section. Later, the global editor assigned detailed Fama-French coverage to a single section, and a local editor kept a concise finding plus a cross-reference. Every step is inspectable — yet the final report still retained an internal section identifier and missed some benchmark requirements. Traceability helps diagnose gaps; it does not guarantee coverage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the honest summary: a practical architecture for keeping research branches independent without losing a shared agenda, evidence that the harness and model contribute separately, and clear boundaries on what the evaluation can support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Links and disclosure
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Technical report: &lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36071" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;arXiv:2609.36071&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Code (harness only): &lt;a href="https://github.com/meituan-longcat/LongCat-DeepResearch" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/meituan-longcat/LongCat-DeepResearch&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Project page: &lt;a href="https://meituan-longcat.github.io/LongCat-DeepResearch/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;meituan-longcat.github.io/LongCat-DeepResearch&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Disclosure: I am an author of the technical report. This post was prepared and checked using AI tools, based on the technical report; it is a personal post, not an official Meituan LongCat team announcement. Only the research harness is released — the model weights, training data, hidden rubrics, trajectories, and training code are not open-sourced.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The software works. It's free indefinitely. People even signed up. Getting them to actually use it? That's another story.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5bqmn07sozeo5m0krr8h.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5bqmn07sozeo5m0krr8h.png" alt=" " width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Two months ago, I launched my SaaS platform.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not a landing page collecting emails. Not a prototype with three buttons that don't work. A real software platform with user accounts, product management, online ordering, payment integration, and operational tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built it with the help of AI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's live, functional, and free for businesses to use. Not a trial. Not a promotional offer. Free indefinitely.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And two months later, I still have zero active customers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the AI SaaS revolution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI Can Build Software. Apparently, It Can't Make People Want It.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You've probably seen the videos:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Build your SaaS in a weekend!"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Launch a startup without knowing how to code!"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Here's how I built a $10K/month SaaS using AI!"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the interesting part: AI really has made software development dramatically easier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can generate code, create interfaces, integrate payment processors, troubleshoot errors, and deploy applications at a fraction of what those capabilities would have cost a few years ago.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's remarkable. I've experienced it firsthand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But there's one detail missing from most of those success stories.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Just because you can build something doesn't mean anyone wants to use it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And even when they say they want it, that doesn't mean they'll actually use it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People Were Interested. Then They Disappeared.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't simply launch a website and expect customers to show up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I contacted potential users, ran advertisements, responded to questions, demonstrated features, and made changes based on feedback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And people did show interest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's what my acquisition funnel looked like:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;43 business owners expressed interest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;8 registered independently.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;1 independently added a product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;0 became active, ongoing users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Numbers to be verified against the actual records before publication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People weren't necessarily rejecting the idea. Some were willing to register and start configuring their businesses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But they weren't completing the journey.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I started investigating where they stopped.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One problem became clear: the onboarding experience was asking people to make decisions before they were ready. Even the wording of a button could make the process feel like a bigger commitment than intended.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I recently redesigned that part of the experience, including making it clearer that people can save their progress without immediately publishing anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Will it fix the problem?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't know yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But I learned something valuable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A signup isn't a customer. A customer isn't necessarily an active user. And an interested prospect isn't proof of demand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Feature Trap&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's another problem nobody warns you about when AI makes development easier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every time someone says, "It would be great if your software could also do this," you can actually build it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So you do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You add another feature, improve a workflow, redesign a screen, or integrate another service. Every improvement feels like progress because technically it is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But here's the uncomfortable question:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Did that new feature bring you a single active customer?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my case, improving the software has been considerably easier than convincing someone to adopt it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Development gives you immediate feedback. You change something, test it, and see the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Customer acquisition is different. You can spend hours reaching out to people, explaining your product, and answering questions, only to receive silence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Naturally, it's tempting to return to development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At least the AI responds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Free Doesn't Eliminate the Cost of Switching&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This has been one of my biggest lessons.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I assumed making the platform free would remove a major obstacle to adoption. But businesses already have ways of doing things, even if those methods are inefficient.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They might use spreadsheets, social media messages, payment apps, handwritten notes, or a combination of all four.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They're familiar with those processes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trying something new means creating an account, learning another interface, configuring settings, adding products, and trusting an unfamiliar platform with part of their business.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's a real cost, even when the software itself costs nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not just competing with other SaaS platforms. I'm competing with the way people already do things.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And apparently, familiarity is a stronger competitor than I expected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Barrier to Building Software Has Collapsed. The Barrier to Selling It Hasn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before AI, creating sophisticated software required substantial technical knowledge, money, time, or access to developers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those requirements created barriers to entry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI has lowered those barriers dramatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now thousands of people can build products in markets they barely understand, and many of those products look surprisingly professional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's wonderful for innovation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But it also means that being able to build software is becoming less of a competitive advantage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your competitors have access to the same tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They can build similar features, launch attractive websites, and integrate the same services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Meanwhile, the difficult parts haven't gone away: finding customers, earning trust, changing habits, simplifying onboarding, and getting people to come back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI has made software easier to produce. It hasn't made customers easier to acquire.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What Two Months Actually Taught Me&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not going to claim that two months without meaningful adoption proves my product has no market.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It doesn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Perhaps my messaging is wrong. Perhaps my audience is wrong. Perhaps people need more help getting started.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But I've learned three things that I wish I'd understood before launching.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, interest is cheap. Someone saying your product looks useful is not the same as someone being willing to change how they operate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Second, onboarding is part of the product. Building great features means little if users never get far enough to experience them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Third, distribution deserves as much attention as development. It's possible to spend months improving software while making almost no progress toward acquiring customers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The technical work is not necessarily the biggest obstacle anymore.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finding people who will actually use what you've built may be.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI SaaS Gold Rush&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's an old saying about gold rushes: the people consistently making money aren't necessarily the miners.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They're the ones selling the shovels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now look at the AI SaaS industry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People buy AI subscriptions, tokens, hosting, domains, development tools, advertising, and courses about building software businesses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They build applications faster than ever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They launch products faster than ever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And many still struggle to get a single customer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the companies selling the tools get paid whether those startups succeed or fail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The economics are fascinating:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI companies make money when you build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hosting companies make money when you deploy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The advertising platforms make money when you promote.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And you make money only when your product actually generates revenue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Notice which one is the least guaranteed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not suggesting AI tools are worthless. Quite the opposite. They've made it possible for me to build software that would otherwise have required substantially more resources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But that doesn't mean building it was a good business decision.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The market gets to decide that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What Happens Next?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm not shutting the platform down.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've identified problems in onboarding and made changes. I'm continuing to talk to potential customers and trying to understand what prevents them from adopting the software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But my goal has changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm no longer measuring success by how many features I release or how much functionality AI helps me implement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My next milestone is one genuine, independently active customer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One business that starts using the software, comes back, and actually relies on it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not a registration. Not a compliment. Not another feature request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One real customer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'll write the follow-up when that happens, or when I learn something significant about why it hasn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Until then, here's my advice to everyone watching those AI SaaS success videos and planning to launch the next million-dollar startup over the weekend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Go ahead and build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But figure out who will use it first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because the market doesn't care how impressive your AI-generated code is. It doesn't care how many features you've implemented or how much you spent generating them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Welcome to the AI SaaS gold rush.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everyone has a shovel. Everyone is digging.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the people making money might just be the ones selling the shovels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;By Bo&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use a Telegram operator workflow to review drafted replies before anything can reach a recipient. The useful part is not the button itself. It is the distinction between a proposed message, a human decision, and evidence that the message was actually sent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One documented revision session made that distinction concrete. I replaced a batch of drafts with corrected wording. The operator approved the replacements, and the approved records reached the outbox. The delivery component was unavailable at that checkpoint. Approval had happened; delivery had not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a normal queue state. Calling it “sent” would be a reporting defect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Separate the records by responsibility
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The recorded workflow uses newline-delimited JSON files between components. A drafts feed holds candidate replies. A revision feed carries operator corrections. An outbox holds approved work. An events feed records delivery outcomes and incoming replies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This separation makes inspection straightforward. I can ask whether a draft exists without implying that it was approved, and inspect an approval without implying that the external platform accepted it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a deliberately simplified state sketch. It describes the responsibilities, not a complete implementation of the existing bot:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;draft -&amp;gt; shown -&amp;gt; approved -&amp;gt; queued -&amp;gt; confirmed sent
\-&amp;gt; revision requested -&amp;gt; new draft
\-&amp;gt; rejected

old shown draft -&amp;gt; replaced
queued -&amp;gt; delivery uncertain -&amp;gt; reconciliation
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The distinction between queued and confirmed sent must survive a worker restart. A friendly Telegram notification cannot substitute for a durable outcome record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Approve a particular version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the revision session, the bot assigned each replacement a new draft identifier. The old cards were closed before the new records were introduced. The bot's callback handler rejected actions on drafts whose stored status was no longer “shown.”&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That server-side check matters because the operator may still have an old card visible on a phone. Editing a card improves the interface, but stored state determines whether its button can authorize anything.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical rule is simple: approval belongs to the version the person reviewed. If the wording changes, create a new version and require a new decision. Do not let a worker edit an already approved body while keeping the approval attached.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same principle applies to recipients and destinations. A review screen should make clear where the exact approved text is intended to go.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Treat Telegram as the interface
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#callbackquery" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Telegram Bot API&lt;/a&gt; provides callback queries for button interactions. Its &lt;a href="https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#answercallbackquery" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;callback acknowledgement method&lt;/a&gt; clears the client's progress indicator. Acknowledging that interaction is separate from recording authorization or completing delivery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a new implementation, I would resolve a short callback reference against server-side state, check the actor's authorization, and verify that the referenced version is still actionable. I would also make the state transition and outbox insertion one recoverable operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are implementation requirements I would verify explicitly. The historical session proves replacement-card handling; it does not establish transactional guarantees under simultaneous callbacks or process crashes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Keep revisions visible
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The operator needs a clear answer to “which text am I approving?” In the documented workflow, a replacement appears as another version, and a superseded card says that its text will not be sent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A revision request is work too. After satisfying it, the drafting component must mark or otherwise reconcile that request. The session notes identified a request that was already satisfied but still present in the revision feed. Consuming it again could have created an unnecessary additional draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I prefer a visible pending revision to a silent rewrite. The former can be inspected; the latter makes it difficult to explain which wording crossed the approval boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Handle delivery uncertainty honestly
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sender can fail before submission, after submission, or while waiting for confirmation. Those cases need different recovery decisions. An outbox row alone cannot tell them apart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a production sender, I would use a stable delivery reference, exclusive claiming, and durable result recording. After an ambiguous timeout, I would reconcile the destination's history before retrying. Where the destination has no idempotency support, I would preserve an explicit uncertain state for review rather than promise exactly-once delivery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a proposed hardening pattern, not a claim that a JSONL file automatically implements it. Multiple writers also need locking or a transactional store. Append-only storage is useful for a timeline, but it does not resolve concurrency by itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Test the boundaries without sending
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Useful local checks include pressing an obsolete card, approving the same version twice, revising after approval, and restarting a worker between queue insertion and outcome recording. A test sender should capture intended output locally, with external delivery disabled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would check the copy as well as the state machine. The revised drafts in the recorded session used first-person wording because the operator was speaking personally. A mechanically valid approval of misleading text is still a poor result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My acceptance question is therefore specific: can I trace this exact message from reviewed version, to authorized decision, to independently observed delivery outcome? If the trace stops at the outbox, I report “approved and queued.” That small wording discipline keeps a convenient Telegram interface from becoming an unreliable account of what happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


How to verify an x402 payment actually settled on-chain
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When an API endpoint charges an AI agent per call, somebody eventually has to answer a simple question: did this payment actually happen?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A dashboard saying "paid" is not an answer. A settlement notification is not an answer. Both are claims — someone, or some system, saying a payment happened. Claims can be early, replayed, mismatched, or just wrong. If you run a paid endpoint, you want proof, per payment, that you can check yourself. Here is the check, in order.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What does "settled" mean for an x402 payment?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An x402 payment on Base, in the EIP-3009 form, is an authorization, not just a transfer. Five things have to line up:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Payer:&lt;/strong&gt; the wallet that authorized the payment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Payee:&lt;/strong&gt; the wallet that should receive it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Amount:&lt;/strong&gt; the exact USDC amount authorized.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nonce:&lt;/strong&gt; a one-time authorization identifier. EIP-3009 authorizations are single-use — once the nonce is consumed, that authorization cannot be spent again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Validity window:&lt;/strong&gt; the time range (validAfter to validBefore) during which the authorization may be used.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Settled" means all five line up on-chain: the right payer authorized the right amount to the right payee, the nonce was actually consumed, and it happened inside the window. Anything less is a claim, not a settlement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Check 1: Does the signature recover to the payer?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take the signed authorization and recover the signer from its signature (ecrecover over the EIP-3009 typed data). The recovered address must equal the authorization's &lt;code&gt;from&lt;/code&gt; field.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If it does not, stop. No other check rescues a payment whose authorization was not signed by the supposed payer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Check 2: Was the nonce actually consumed on-chain?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Query the USDC contract's &lt;code&gt;authorizationState&lt;/code&gt; for the payer and nonce. It must read consumed (true).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the check most people skip, and it is the one that catches the "it said paid, but nothing moved" case. A signed authorization sitting unspent is not a payment. An unconsumed nonce means the payment has not settled, whatever any notification said.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Check 3: Does the settlement transfer match the authorization?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Find the settlement transaction's USDC Transfer log and compare it, field by field, against the authorization: from, to, and value must match exactly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A transfer for the wrong amount to the wrong address is not your payment, even if it arrived at about the right time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Check 4: Did it settle inside the validity window?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take the settled block's timestamp and confirm it falls inside [validAfter, validBefore].&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A payment that settles outside its window is not a valid use of that authorization, even if the money moved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four checks, all passing, is a verified settlement. Anything else is failed or unverified — and that distinction should be stored next to the payment, not smoothed over.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Worked example: one real $0.005 settlement, check by check
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a real settlement from the public log at &lt;a href="https://demo.quinnsconveyance.com/insights" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://demo.quinnsconveyance.com/insights&lt;/a&gt; — a $0.005 USDC payment on Base, payer &lt;code&gt;0xC3ADe26717A60ff29875E3b17f016c1fC75672B9&lt;/code&gt;, payee &lt;code&gt;0x3a97d5F616D26876020568bD631492F59cFB428C&lt;/code&gt;, settled October 19, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Transaction: &lt;a href="https://basescan.org/tx/0x266847d9f6085e6756f04d791c8256c67749e89493a8da5671578caab733988d" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://basescan.org/tx/0x266847d9f6085e6756f04d791c8256c67749e89493a8da5671578caab733988d&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check 1, signature:&lt;/strong&gt; PASS. The signature recovers to the payer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check 2, nonce consumed:&lt;/strong&gt; on recheck, PASS. The authorization's nonce reads consumed on-chain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check 3, transfer match:&lt;/strong&gt; PASS. The settlement transaction's USDC Transfer log matches payer, payee, and 5000 (the smallest USDC units for $0.005) exactly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check 4, validity window:&lt;/strong&gt; on recheck, PASS. The settled block timestamp falls inside the authorization's window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Verdict on recheck: verified, 4/4.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I am showing the recheck verdict deliberately, because the first pass on this same settlement did not look like that — and that is the next section.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why a first check can fail and a recheck can pass
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On this settlement's first pass, checks 1 and 3 passed, but checks 2 and 4 failed. The payment was real. The money had moved. What had not happened yet was chain-state propagation: verification ran at settlement time, before the chain state it reads had caught up, so the nonce check ran against a chain that had not yet recorded the consumption, and the window check ran against state that was not yet stable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That pattern repeated across the whole batch. In the first pass over 16 logged settlements, 11 came back failed, 1 unverified, and 4 verified — and in every one of the 12 non-verified rows, exactly the same two checks failed (authorizationState and window), while signature and transfer match passed every time. There was not a single first-pass signature failure or transfer mismatch in the set. On recheck, all 16 verified, 4/4.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is why verification cannot be a one-shot at settlement time. Quinn's Conveyance runs an automatic recheck at +10 minutes, and stores the recheck result alongside the first pass. A system that only checks once, immediately, will mark real payments as failed — and a system that quietly re-runs until it passes, without keeping the first result, will hide the timing problem entirely. Keep both results. The first pass tells you about timing; the recheck tells you about truth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What to do when a check fails
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not silently accept the payment, and do not silently drop it either. Record it as failed or unverified, surface it, and let a human (or a recheck) decide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule I use, and the one I argued for publicly in my earlier verifier post: fail-open, but loud. Verification never blocks a settlement — if the verifier is down, the payment still lands — but it is recorded as unverified, permanently, not passed in silence. "Unverified" is honest. Silence is not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And the same rule applies to the status convention itself: every settlement should carry a status — verified, failed, or unverified. Statuses, yes. Permission, no. A status is a trust signal; making it a gatekeeper would make the verifier a single point of failure for the whole ecosystem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is a 402 response proof of payment?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
No. A 402 response is a payment request. It proves an endpoint asked to be paid, not that it was.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is a facilitator's confirmation enough?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
It is a claim to verify. A facilitator saying "settled" is useful, but it is the same kind of statement as a dashboard saying "paid." Run the four checks against the chain before you count the revenue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How long should I wait before rechecking?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Long enough for chain state to propagate. Quinn's uses +10 minutes for its automatic recheck. The exact number matters less than the principle: check once at settlement, then check again after the state you are reading has had time to settle. If the second check passes and the first did not, you had a timing problem, not a fraud problem — and you now have the record to prove which one it was.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can I verify without a transaction hash?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Partially. Without a tx hash, checks 3 and 4 (transfer match and window) cannot run, and the verdict rests on signature recovery plus nonce consumption. That is still much better than a notification — but it is not a full verification. Get the tx hash when you can.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it on a real log
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The public settlement log, with its verify_status and Recheck columns, is live at &lt;a href="https://demo.quinnsconveyance.com/insights" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://demo.quinnsconveyance.com/insights&lt;/a&gt;. Pick any row, run the four checks above against its Basescan link, and see whether you land on the same verdict. That is the whole point: verification you can redo yourself is the only kind worth trusting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;— John O'Malley, Quinn's Conveyance. I install this verification layer, alongside settlement logging, on paid API endpoints — &lt;a href="mailto:john@quinnsconveyance.com"&gt;john@quinnsconveyance.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Automated Multi‑Platform Publishing Pipeline – How I Fixed the Content‑Automation Breakage
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; A stale &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; flag and a missing Bluesky payload stopped our weekly auto‑publish. By synchronizing the generation flags, adding the missing &lt;code&gt;bluesky_en.json&lt;/code&gt;, and tightening the commit flow, the pipeline now pushes to Medium, Substack and Bluesky in a single CI run.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our &lt;code&gt;content-automation&lt;/code&gt; repo drives the weekly “Build‑in‑Public” posts that go out to Medium, Substack, Bluesky and a private Slack channel. On 2026‑10‑22 the CI job that runs &lt;code&gt;npm run generate&lt;/code&gt; started failing with:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Error: Unable to locate content/2026/10/21/content-automation/bluesky_en.json
at src/publish/bluesky.js:45:13
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The generator printed &lt;em&gt;“Skipping Bluesky publish – no payload found”&lt;/em&gt; and the &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; still reported &lt;code&gt;"bluesky_published": false&lt;/code&gt;. Meanwhile, Medium and Substack posts were being created but the &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; flags &lt;code&gt;medium_generated&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;substack_generated&lt;/code&gt; remained &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;, causing downstream scripts to think the week’s content hadn’t been published at all. The result was a half‑finished release that required manual intervention.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first instinct was to patch the CI script to ignore the missing Bluesky file:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# CI step – naive fallback&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-f&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$payload_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;then
&lt;/span&gt;node src/publish/bluesky.js &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$payload_path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;else
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Bluesky payload missing – continuing"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;fi&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That let the job finish, but the &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; still reported &lt;code&gt;bluesky_published: false&lt;/code&gt;, so the “publish‑status” dashboard kept flagging the week as incomplete. I also tried flipping the flags manually in &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; before the CI run:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"medium_generated"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"substack_generated"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"bluesky_published"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That solved the dashboard noise but broke the idempotency guarantee – a subsequent run would think the content was already published and skip regeneration entirely. Neither approach addressed the root cause: the generator never produced the Bluesky JSON payload, and the metadata flags were out of sync with the actual file system state.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Generate the missing Bluesky payload
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The generator lives in &lt;code&gt;src/generator/bluesky.js&lt;/code&gt;. I added a deterministic output path and ensured the file is always written, even if the content is empty:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/generator/bluesky.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;buildBlueskyPayload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;article&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;progress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;article&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;summary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;tags&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;article&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tags&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;outDir&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content-automation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;outFile&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;bluesky_en.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Ensure directory exists&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mkdirSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outDir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;recursive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;outFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;outFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;buildBlueskyPayload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Running the generator now creates &lt;code&gt;content/2026/10/22/content-automation/bluesky_en.json&lt;/code&gt; automatically. The new file appears in the repo with the exact diff you can see in the commit:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+[
+  {
+    "type": "progress",
+    "text": "Finally fixed the bug in content/2026/10/21/content-automation/bluesky_en.json that was preventing the auto‑generated 2026‑10‑22 post from being published on Bluesky.",
+    "tags": ["buildinpublic","automation"],
+    "date": "2026-10-22"
+  }
+]
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


2. Keep &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; in lockstep
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of manually editing &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;, I introduced a small helper &lt;code&gt;src/utils/metadata.js&lt;/code&gt; that atomically updates the JSON after each successful publish step:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/utils/metadata.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;content/2026/10/22/content-automation/metadata.json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMeta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;utf8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;updateMeta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;patch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMeta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;patch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Write atomically to avoid race conditions&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadMeta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updateMeta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Each publisher now calls &lt;code&gt;updateMeta&lt;/code&gt; on success:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/publish/bluesky.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updateMeta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;../utils/metadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;publishSuccess&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;updateMeta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;bluesky_published&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;bluesky_uris&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;uri&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The same pattern was applied to Medium and Substack publishers, resulting in a clean &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; after the run:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -12,8 +12,14 @@&lt;/span&gt;
"pull_requests": 0,
"releases": 0,
"closed_issues": 0,
&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-  "medium_generated": false,
-  "substack_generated": false,
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "medium_generated": true,
+  "substack_generated": true,
&lt;/span&gt;   "bluesky_published": true,
&lt;span class="gd"&gt;-  "bluesky_uris": {},
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "bluesky_uris": {
+    "2026-10-22": "https://bsky.app/profile/roberto.luna/post/3kz6..."
+  },
+  "last_run": "2026-10-23T04:12:00Z"
&lt;/span&gt; }
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


3. Tighten the CI pipeline
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CI job now runs three sequential steps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Generate&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;code&gt;npm run generate&lt;/code&gt; (creates markdown files and &lt;code&gt;bluesky_en.json&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Publish&lt;/strong&gt; – Calls each publisher (&lt;code&gt;node src/publish/medium.js&lt;/code&gt;, etc.) and relies on the atomic metadata updates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Validate&lt;/strong&gt; – A new script &lt;code&gt;npm run verify&lt;/code&gt; reads &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; and exits with a non‑zero code if any flag is &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# .github/workflows/publish.yml&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;jobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;publish&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;runs-on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;ubuntu-latest&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;steps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;uses&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;actions/checkout@v3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Install&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;npm ci&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Generate content&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;npm run generate&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Publish to platforms&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="pi"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;node src/publish/medium.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;node src/publish/substack.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s"&gt;node src/publish/bluesky.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Verify publish status&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;npm run verify&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the verification step fails, the workflow aborts and alerts the team, preventing silent failures like the one we experienced.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never let a &lt;strong&gt;state flag&lt;/strong&gt; become the source of truth without a &lt;strong&gt;single source of truth&lt;/strong&gt; for the underlying artifact. By generating the payload first, then atomically updating &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; after each successful publish, you guarantee that the flag truly reflects reality and you avoid “phantom‑published” weeks.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What's Next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’m adding a &lt;strong&gt;schema‑validation&lt;/strong&gt; step for each payload (using &lt;code&gt;ajv&lt;/code&gt;), so malformed JSON will fail fast before any API call. Additionally, I plan&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-10-23&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;A presales war story about a blacklist alert that hit every server at once — the sign that nothing is wrong with the servers — and the one DNS query I now run before anyone touches a mail config.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The alert came in on a Saturday morning. Our blacklist monitor showed three servers going from 0 to 1. Three different servers, three different jobs, three different owners inside the company.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Within minutes the first message arrived in the team chat.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"All three are on a blacklist. We must be compromised."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is a fair fear. A listing is usually what a hack looks like from the outside. I asked one question first.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Did they go from clean to listed at different times, or all at once?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Silence. Then: "All at once. Same check."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That was the answer. Three unrelated servers do not get hacked in the same minute. Something above the servers had changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Trap, Named
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the trap. When every server alerts together, the instinct is to open the servers. Check the logs. Check the mail queue. Reset passwords.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It all feels like work. &lt;strong&gt;It is all work on the wrong layer.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The listing was not about the servers. It was about the network they sit in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What a UCEPROTECT Level 3 Listing Actually Is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;UCEPROTECT runs a blacklist in three levels. The level tells you what is being listed:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Level 1&lt;/strong&gt; — one single IP address that sent spam to their trap.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Level 2&lt;/strong&gt; — a whole range of addresses of one provider.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Level 3&lt;/strong&gt; — a whole ASN. Every IP the network announces.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An ASN is the identity of a network on the internet. It is the number that says "this block of addresses belongs to this operator". A Level 3 listing says: &lt;strong&gt;too many addresses inside this network behaved badly, so we list all of them.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your server can be perfect and still be listed. It shares the number with everyone else in the network.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is common in three places:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A hosting provider or ISP with many customers on one ASN.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A company with an old public range that mixes mail servers, VPN users and customer lines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Any network where nobody owns the question "who sends mail from this range?"&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The listing page gave me the numbers. A spam score of 168.8. Six Level 1 abuser addresses seen in the last seven days. And a count of 54 "impacts" against an escalation limit of 50.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last line is the whole mechanism. The limit was 50. The network sat at 54. Four impacts over, and the entire ASN was listed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Nobody Sees It Coming
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two reasons the usual checks miss this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, your own server tests show nothing. The server's own address is not listed at Level 1 or Level 2. A mail test tool that checks only the IP may show a clean result, or one confusing result among many.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Second, the cause is not on your servers at all. It is five or six addresses somewhere else in the same network, owned by people who never read your alerts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Absence of a listing on your IP is not health. It only tells you the problem is above you.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to Confirm It — and Fix the Right Layer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part that takes ten minutes, and it saves a day of looking in the wrong place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Step 1: Check the Level 3 list first.&lt;/strong&gt; UCEPROTECT is queried by IP, with the octets reversed. For the address 192.0.2.10 the query is:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dig +short 10.2.0.192.dnsbl-3.uceprotect.net
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the answer is &lt;code&gt;127.0.0.2&lt;/code&gt;, the address is covered by a Level 3 listing. No answer means it is not. Run the same query for Level 1 and Level 2 on the same address:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dig +short 10.2.0.192.dnsbl-1.uceprotect.net
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dig +short 10.2.0.192.dnsbl-2.uceprotect.net
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In my case Level 3 answered and Level 1 and Level 2 did not. That combination is the proof: the server itself is not listed, its network is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Step 2: Confirm which ASN you are in.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dig +short TXT 10.2.0.192.origin.asn.cymru.com
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first number in the answer is the ASN. Check that all three alerting servers show the same one. If they do, you are looking at the same listing three times, not three incidents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Step 3: Find the abuser addresses.&lt;/strong&gt; Level 3 appears when Level 1 hits pile up inside the ASN. So list every address your network announces, and ask the Level 1 list about each one. I took the prefixes from the public RIPEstat data, which gave 32,768 addresses, and ran the DNS query against all of them with asynchronous lookups, 256 at a time. It finished in a few minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Five addresses were listed. Four of them were addresses nobody in the team recognised — one carried only the generic default reverse name of the provider. The fifth was a surprise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It was the company's own mail server.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nobody had been looking at it, because everyone assumed the problem was out there. A sixth abuser had already dropped off Level 1 by the time I scanned, which matches the six in the last-seven-days count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Step 4: Fix it on the network side, not the server side.&lt;/strong&gt; This is the step that is different from every other blacklist article. The people who can act are the network operations and abuse team. They can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find out which customers own the listed addresses and stop the spam.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fix the company's own mail server, if it is on the list.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Stop Level 1 hits from accumulating again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Step 5: Let it expire.&lt;/strong&gt; Level 3 listings clear on their own once the seven-day impact count falls under the limit. The listing page does not show a start date, so watch the count, not the calendar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Never pay for express delisting.&lt;/strong&gt; The paid removal clears the symptom and teaches the list that you will pay. The abusers are still in your network, and the count climbs again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plain takeaway: a Level 3 listing is fixed by cleaning five addresses, not by touching three servers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Bigger Lesson — Who Owns the Range?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The technical fix took one afternoon. The real gap was organisational, and it is the part I raise in the next architecture review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nobody owned the question "what sends mail from our address space?" The company's own mail server was on the list for a reason that nobody had looked at. Nobody could say which addresses in the range were allowed to send directly on port 25. Each team watched its own server and nobody watched the range.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the short list I leave behind after a case like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Know your ASN and your prefixes.&lt;/strong&gt; Write them down. Check them against the public registry data once a month.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Monitor the range, not only the servers.&lt;/strong&gt; One scan of the whole range against the Level 1 list takes minutes. Run it on a schedule.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Block outbound port 25 from customer lines.&lt;/strong&gt; Allow it only from the servers that are meant to send mail.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Give every sending server a proper reverse name.&lt;/strong&gt; A generic default name is how an address ends up looking like a customer line.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Alert on "same minute" patterns.&lt;/strong&gt; Several servers changing state together is a network event until proven otherwise.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plain takeaway: a blacklist is a signal about behaviour you can see. Make sure someone is looking at all of it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One Other Thing I Saw That Day
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While checking, two other lists flagged the same servers. They looked like part of the same event. They were not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One list flags addresses when the registry record for the country domain has no owner email. That one is old and unrelated.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One score service answered with &lt;code&gt;127.255.255.255&lt;/code&gt;. That is not a listing. It means "your query was refused", which happens when you send DNS queries through a large public resolver.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both are noise. If you do not tell the noise from the real listing, you chase three problems and fix none.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Tell Every Customer Now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When more than one server alerts at the same time, I ask the team this before anyone logs in:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"Did the alerts start in the same minute?"&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the answer is yes, I run the Level 3 query on one address first. It takes ten seconds. It tells us if we should be looking at the servers or at the network.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the answer is "different days", then it is probably the servers, and the normal checklist applies. If the answer is "same minute", we are not done until someone has looked at the ASN.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I write about the questions I ask in real customer rooms — the ones that change architectures and surface the problems no one else noticed. If you found this useful, drop your email below and I'll send the next one when it's ready.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What is a UCEPROTECT Level 3 listing?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is a blacklist entry that covers a whole ASN, which means every IP address a network announces. It appears when enough addresses inside that network have been listed at Level 1 within seven days. Your own server does not need to have sent any spam.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I know if my server is listed because of its own IP or its ASN?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Query the same address against the Level 1, Level 2 and Level 3 lists. If only Level 3 answers with 127.0.0.2, the listing is on the ASN. If Level 1 answers, the address itself is listed and you should look at that server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How long does a Level 3 listing last?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It clears on its own once the number of Level 1 impacts in the last seven days falls under the escalation limit. In my case the limit was 50 and the network was at 54. The listing page does not show a start date, so watch the count.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Should I pay for express delisting?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Paying removes the entry but not the cause. The abusing addresses are still in your network, so the count rises again and the listing returns. Fix the addresses that caused the hits instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does a Level 3 listing stop my email?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only for receivers that use this list to reject mail. Many large providers do not. The risk is with smaller receivers and some corporate gateways, so watch for bounces that name the list and tell affected customers early.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Who can fix a Level 3 listing?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The network operator. The operations or abuse team can find the listed addresses, contact the customers behind them, and fix the operator's own mail servers. A single customer or server owner cannot clear it alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://quabin.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/quabin-uceprotect-level-3-asn-blacklist-all-servers.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Download the PDF&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://quabin.com/uceprotect-level-3-asn-blacklist-all-servers/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;quabin.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One-to-one chats are the easy case. Group chats are where key management gets genuinely hard, because the group itself changes. Someone joins. Someone leaves. Someone's phone is replaced. Every one of those events has to change what the group can read — without breaking the messages already in flight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post is about how my protocol, PQ-MPRBA, handles that: a small state machine called the Group Epoch FSM.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The two promises a group chat has to keep
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a member leaves, they must not be able to read future messages. That is forward secrecy for groups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a member joins, they must not be able to read past messages. That is backward secrecy for groups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both sound obvious. Both are easy to get wrong. The classic failure is rotating the key but letting old messages stay decryptable with the old key, or rotating so aggressively that half the group loses messages during the transition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Epochs instead of one key
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of one group key, the group moves through epochs. Each epoch has its own key material, and the group is always in one of three states:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;STABLE&lt;/strong&gt; — normal chatting, one current epoch.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ROTATING&lt;/strong&gt; — a membership change has triggered a rotation, and the new epoch is being distributed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;STABLE&lt;/strong&gt; again — everyone has the new epoch, and the old one is retired.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The state machine is deliberately boring. There are no clever shortcuts, just three states and clear transitions. In security code, boring is a feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The grace buffer that saves your messages
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tricky part is the transition. When rotation starts, some messages encrypted under the old epoch are still traveling through the network. If you delete the old key the instant rotation begins, those messages arrive undecryptable and the chat looks broken.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the protocol keeps a grace buffer of three epochs. During rotation, the group can still decrypt messages from the previous epochs while everyone moves to the new one. Once the transition is complete and the buffer slides forward, the old keys are wiped — with the same zeroization discipline as the one-to-one ratchet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three epochs is a judgment call, not a law of nature. One would break messages. Ten would keep old keys alive too long. Three covers a realistic transition window without turning the buffer into an archive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why this matters beyond groups
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same pattern shows up everywhere keys have a lifetime: sessions, devices, teams. The lesson is general — a key rotation is not an event, it is a transition, and transitions need a defined state machine, a bounded grace period, and a real deletion at the end. Skip any of the three and you get either broken messages or keys that never die.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What is still honest to say
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As with the rest of PQ-MPRBA, this is experimental work by one person, not independently audited. The group rotation is implemented and used in my messenger, but I will not claim it has survived adversarial review. The design is public so it can be criticized — that is the point of publishing it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code is open source: &lt;a href="https://github.com/indirect-b/chat-app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/indirect-b/chat-app&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you have built group key rotation yourself, I would love to hear how you sized your grace window — that is the number I am least sure about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A travel brief often mixes the trip itself with meeting context, preferences, and unresolved decisions. A short summary can help the person review it before requesting proposed form changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a useful place for Chrome’s Summarizer API: a bounded reading aid beside an existing text field.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Trip Workbench&lt;/a&gt;, the public demo I built and maintain, the original brief already feeds a separate Gemini feature that proposes trip fields for review. The local summary has a different purpose. It helps the person read the brief; it does not update the trip, replace the source text, or decide which details the cloud model receives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This walkthrough builds on &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/tree/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;commit &lt;code&gt;4b3f370&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. The demo has the basic API call. The lifecycle extensions below add capability states, download feedback, cancellation, and a boundary that prevents an old summary from appearing beside a changed brief.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Choose a reading task with a clear boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider this synthetic brief:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I need to attend a customer workshop in New York on November 12–13, 2026. Depart SFO for JFK on November 11. Economy, one traveler. Do not book a hotel because I will stay with family. Travel only if the client confirms the workshop by Friday. Plan to return November 14, but November 15 is still possible if the workshop extends.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful summary would preserve the dates, the hotel exclusion, the confirmation condition, and the uncertainty around the return. A fluent summary that converts those details into a confirmed itinerary would be misleading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the original visible beside the output. Label the result “Summary of this brief” and let the person regenerate it after editing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Continue sending the &lt;strong&gt;original brief&lt;/strong&gt; to Gemini when the person selects “Suggest trip details.” Using a lossy summary as the extraction input would make any omitted condition harder for the next step to recover.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The feature needs no application API key or &lt;code&gt;@google/genai&lt;/code&gt; call. Chrome supplies the on-device model. Chrome documents that model use does not send the input to Google or a third party, while initial model setup can require a download. That statement applies to the local inference path; the application’s separate Gemini request remains a cloud operation. See &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/summarizer-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Chrome’s Summarizer guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Treat availability as part of the interface
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome documents Summarizer support from version 138, subject to platform, hardware, storage, and model availability. It remains a limited-availability web API, so browser version alone is insufficient. Serve the feature in a &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Summarizer" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;secure context&lt;/a&gt; and retain the ordinary text workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are two checks: whether the API exists, and whether it can support the configuration you want.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use the same core options for checking availability and creating the instance. This example requests English key points in plain text. The existing demo uses a short &lt;code&gt;tldr&lt;/code&gt;; medium key points give this review task more room without guaranteeing that every detail survives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The examples use JavaScript and JSX. For TypeScript, Chrome recommends &lt;a href="https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/tree/master/types/dom-chromium-ai" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;@types/dom-chromium-ai&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. Replace the demo’s handwritten Summarizer global declaration if adopting those types.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;summary-client.js&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;summaryOptions&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;key-points&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;plain-text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;medium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;expectedInputLanguages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;outputLanguage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;en&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;checkSummaryAvailability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;API&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;globalThis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Summarizer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;API&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;unsupported&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;API&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;availability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;summaryOptions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;available&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;downloadable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;downloading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;unavailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Map the &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Summarizer/availability_static" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;availability result&lt;/a&gt; to a useful UI state:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;State&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What the interface should do&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Checking&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keep the brief editable while checking support&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unsupported or unavailable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explain that on-device summarization cannot run here&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Downloadable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Offer the action and explain that model setup may require a download&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Downloading&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Offer to wait for readiness and show progress once attached&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Available&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Offer summarization immediately&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unknown or error&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Offer a readiness retry and preserve the brief&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run this capability check in a parent Effect with a cleanup guard so a late result cannot update an abandoned panel. Do not call &lt;code&gt;create()&lt;/code&gt; from that Effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome’s &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/get-started" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;setup guidance&lt;/a&gt; requires user interaction when model downloads are needed. Let the Summarize button call creation directly. Separating the readiness probe also keeps a potentially slow check out of the click path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Availability can change. A successful probe is not a reservation; creation still needs error handling. When the language or summary configuration changes, check the new options again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Own the summarizer for one request
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For this occasional button action, one instance per request keeps resource ownership straightforward. Create it, generate the summary, and release it in &lt;code&gt;finally&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add this function to the same module:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;summarizeBrief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;onUpdate&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;API&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;globalThis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Summarizer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;throwIfAborted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;API&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;On-device summarization is unavailable.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Enter a brief first.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;API&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;summaryOptions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;monitor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;monitor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;monitor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;downloadprogress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;aborted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onUpdate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;downloading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;percent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;round&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;loaded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;throwIfAborted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;onUpdate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;summarizing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;summarize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;throwIfAborted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;finally&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;session&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;destroy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The signal goes to both &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Summarizer/create_static" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;create()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;code&gt;summarize()&lt;/code&gt;. Cancelling while creation is pending must reach that operation, too. Aborting a creation signal after it resolves also destroys the resulting instance, so this signal belongs to this one request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The final abort check prevents a late result from being accepted after cancellation. The &lt;code&gt;finally&lt;/code&gt; block releases any instance that was acquired, including when generation fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Download progress and generation are separate phases. The &lt;a href="https://webmachinelearning.github.io/writing-assistance-apis/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Writing Assistance API specification&lt;/a&gt; defines download progress as a fraction with a total of 1. A displayed 100% means the download stage reached completion; the interface should still wait for instance creation and summarization to finish.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Destroying an instance releases its resources. It does not mean that the browser removes its downloaded model, nor does this code promise to cancel a shared download for every other page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Batch output keeps this first implementation small. Streaming can improve perceived responsiveness for longer output, but it also requires managing incomplete text and clearing partial results when the request becomes stale.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Bind the result to the brief that produced it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current demo creates an AbortController inside its click handler but does not retain it for cancellation. It also stores the resulting text without binding it to a brief version.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A person can therefore edit the brief while a summary is running, or start another request before the first finishes. The last response to arrive can overwrite the display even when it belongs to older input.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A dedicated summary component can own one active controller. This hook cancels earlier work and accepts updates only from the currently active request:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;summarizeBrief&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;./summary-client.js&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useBriefSummary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;view&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setView&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;idle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setView&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;cancelled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AbortController&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;isCurrent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;aborted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setView&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;preparing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;summarizeBrief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;onUpdate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isCurrent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setView&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isCurrent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setView&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setView&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;aborted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;cancelled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;QuotaExceededError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;This brief is too long. Choose a smaller section.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Could not summarize on this device. Try again or keep reading the brief.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;finally&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;view&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The hook controls request ordering within one mounted summary component. Bind that component’s identity to the source so an edit also clears an already completed summary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trip Workbench already tracks &lt;code&gt;briefVersion&lt;/code&gt;, editor generation, and session generation. Use them with the draft ID:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;summarySourceKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sessionGeneration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;editorGeneration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;savedDraft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;briefVersion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BriefSummary&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;summarySourceKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;readiness&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;readiness&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;React &lt;a href="https://react.dev/learn/preserving-and-resetting-state" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;resets component state when its key changes&lt;/a&gt;. That removes the previous output, and the hook’s cleanup aborts its request. Keep the textarea in the parent so typing retains focus.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using the existing brief version also invalidates a result when the person edits and then restores the same text. If summary options become editable, include their version in this identity as well.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The availability probe can stay in the parent, since changing the brief does not change this example’s fixed language configuration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Show useful states and preserve the source
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inside &lt;code&gt;BriefSummary&lt;/code&gt;, call the hook and derive whether work is in progress:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;view&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cancel&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useBriefSummary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;busy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;preparing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;downloading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;summarizing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;view&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canRun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;available&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;downloadable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;downloading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;readiness&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;aria-label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"Brief summary"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"button"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;disabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;canRun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;busy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;brief&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
